<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wenxiang</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1818">1818</a>–<a href="/years/1876">1876</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Wen-hsiang</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wenxiang was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchu</a> statesman of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a>, a bannerman of the Plain Red Banner, and one of the architects of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/self-strengthening-movement">Self-Strengthening Movement</a>. After gaining the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jinshi">jinshi</a>, the highest degree of the imperial examination, in 1845, he joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-council-qing">Grand Council</a> in 1858. When the Xianfeng Emperor fled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> during the Second Opium War, he stayed and took part in the negotiations with the British and French. A founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zongli-yamen">Zongli Yamen</a>, he was instrumental in devising the government's policy of cooperation with the Western powers between 1861 and 1876.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wenxiang.jpg" alt="Wenxiang" width="320" height="419" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: John Thomson · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wen_Xiang.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
